(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-2-physiology-of-vision-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Physiology of Vision — Physiology Exam 2 — Set 2","category":"Physiology","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"What type of electrical signal do most retinal neurons (other than ganglion cells) use to transmit information, and why is this suited to encoding light intensity?","choices":["Electrotonic (graded) potentials, which allow conduction of signal strength proportional to light intensity","Only chemical neurotransmitter diffusion, bypassing any electrical membrane potential change entirely","Action potentials exclusively, which are all-or-none regardless of light intensity level","No electrical signaling occurs in the retina until signals reach the ganglion cell layer"],"answer":0,"exp":"Most retinal neural elements use electrotonic (graded) potentials rather than action potentials to move current along their membranes, allowing graded conduction of signal strength proportional to light intensity — ganglion cells (and some amacrine cells) are the exception, using action potentials. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Do retinal ganglion cells fire only when stimulated by light, or do they have baseline activity?","choices":["They are spontaneously active with continuous background action potentials, upon which visual signals are superimposed","They only fire briefly at light onset, remaining completely silent otherwise regardless of stimulus","They never generate action potentials, transmitting information only through graded electrotonic potentials","They fire only in complete darkness, becoming entirely silent as soon as light intensity increases"],"answer":0,"exp":"Retinal ganglion cells are spontaneously active with continuous background action potentials; visual signals are superimposed on this background activity, and many ganglion cells are excited by changes in light intensity, responding especially to contrast borders. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What retinal cell type mediates lateral inhibition, and what effect does this have on visual contrast?","choices":["Horizontal cells provide inhibitory feedback to rods, cones, and bipolar cells, preventing lateral spread of light excitation and enhancing visual contrast (excitatory center, inhibitory surround)","Bipolar cells alone provide lateral excitatory feedback that spreads light signals broadly across the retina, actively reducing overall visual contrast","Amacrine cells alone mediate the lateral spread of excitation, allowing signals to blend broadly together and reducing the sharpness of contrast borders","Ganglion cells provide excitatory feedback to the nearby bipolar cells which amplifies the lateral spread of light, substantially decreasing overall edge sharpness in the image"],"answer":0,"exp":"Horizontal cells provide inhibitory feedback (always inhibitory) to rods, cones, and bipolar cells, preventing lateral spread of light excitation across the retina — this creates an excitatory center/inhibitory surround pattern that enhances visual contrast. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What is the optic disc, and why does it create a blind spot?","choices":["The point where the suspensory ligaments attach the lens to the ciliary body around its full circumference","A structure with no real functional significance, containing a dense population of both rods and cones","The point where ganglion cell axons exit the eye to form the optic nerve; it creates a blind spot because there are no rods or cones there","The very centre of the fovea, which contains only cones and produces the single sharpest area of visual acuity anywhere in the retina"],"answer":2,"exp":"The optic disc (optic nerve head) is where approximately 1 million ganglion cell axons exit the eye to form the optic nerve (and where retinal blood vessels enter); because there are no rods or cones at this location, it creates a natural blind spot in each eye. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What is a common functional role of amacrine cells in retinal processing?","choices":["They are interneurons (about 30 different types) whose primary targets are ganglion cells; some respond to signal onset, some to signal extinguishment, and some to movement of light across the retina","They only transmit their signals directly onward to the optic nerve fibres, entirely bypassing both the bipolar and ganglion cells without carrying out any meaningful signal processing","They have no role in visual signal processing at all, functioning solely as structural support cells within the outer nuclear layer of the retina","They exclusively detect color information, with about 3 distinct subtypes each tuned to a single cone photopigment and no other retinal function at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"Amacrine cells are a type of interneuron (about 30 different types) whose primary targets are ganglion cells; different amacrine cell subtypes respond to the onset of a visual signal, the extinguishment of a signal, or the movement of a light signal across the retina, contributing to early visual signal analysis. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"How do P (parvocellular) and M (magnocellular) ganglion cells differ functionally?","choices":["Both the P and M cell types are functionally identical, projecting to the very same magnocellular LGN layers with exactly matched receptive field sizes and conduction speeds","P cells have small receptive fields, slow conduction, and are sensitive to color/fine detail; M cells have larger receptive fields, fast conduction, and are more sensitive to low-contrast black-and-white stimuli","Only M cells project to the lateral geniculate nucleus at all, while P cells instead project exclusively to the superior colliculus for reflexive eye movements","P cells have large receptive fields and fast conduction with complete color insensitivity; M cells have small receptive fields and detect fine color detail"],"answer":1,"exp":"P (parvocellular) ganglion cells have small receptive fields, slow impulse conduction, and are sensitive to color and fine detail (relatively insensitive to low-contrast signals), projecting to parvocellular LGN layers; M (magnocellular) ganglion cells have larger receptive fields, fast conduction, and are more sensitive to low-contrast black-and-white stimuli, projecting to magnocellular LGN layers. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What happens to nerve fibers from the nasal versus temporal retina at the optic chiasm?","choices":["Fibers from the nasal half of each retina cross to the opposite side and join fibers from the opposite eye's temporal retina to form the optic tracts; temporal fibers do not cross","All of the fibres from both the nasal and the temporal retina cross completely over to the opposite side, leaving essentially no uncrossed fibres whatsoever","No fibers cross at the optic chiasm at all; each resulting optic tract instead carries signals from only a single entire eye's retina","Only fibers from the temporal retina cross over to the opposite side, joining the nasal fibers of that same eye to form each optic tract"],"answer":0,"exp":"At the optic chiasm, fibers from the nasal halves of each retina cross to the opposite side, joining uncrossed fibers from the opposite eye's temporal retina to form the optic tracts — allowing each hemisphere to process the corresponding half of the visual field from both eyes. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What are the two principal functions of the dorsal lateral geniculate nucleus in the visual pathway?","choices":["Only relaying information onward to the primary visual cortex, with those pathways bypassing the optic radiation entirely","Relaying information to the primary visual cortex via the optic radiation, and \"gate control\" of information passing to the visual cortex","Regenerating rhodopsin for the rods and cones of the retina via a direct feedback loop with the choroid","Directly generating the full conscious visual perception before any signal even reaches the occipital cortex"],"answer":1,"exp":"The dorsal lateral geniculate nucleus (LGN) of the thalamus has two principal functions: relaying visual information to the primary visual cortex via the optic radiation, and \"gate control\" — regulating how much information passes through to the cortex. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"How are signals from the two eyes organized within the LGN's layered structure?","choices":["Signals from both of the eyes are completely mixed together within every one of the LGN's six distinct cellular layers simultaneously","Signals from the two eyes are kept separate — layers 2, 3, and 5 receive input from the ipsilateral eye, while layers 1, 4, and 6 receive input from the contralateral eye","All six layers of the LGN receive input exclusively from the ipsilateral eye, with no contralateral input reaching it at all","Only signals from the contralateral eye ever reach the LGN, since all ipsilateral fibers terminate earlier at the optic chiasm"],"answer":1,"exp":"In the LGN, signals from the two eyes are kept segregated by layer: layers 2, 3, and 5 receive input from the ipsilateral eye, while layers 1, 4, and 6 receive input from the contralateral eye — separation that is lost once signals reach the primary visual cortex. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Where is the primary visual cortex located, and how is the fovea represented there compared to peripheral retina?","choices":["In the parietal lobe near the postcentral gyrus, with the peripheral retina occupying nearly all of the available cortical surface area","In the calcarine fissure of the occipital lobe, with the fovea (part of the macula) having several hundred times more cortical representation than an equivalent area of peripheral retina","The primary visual cortex has essentially no topographic organisation at all, with the fovea and the periphery randomly intermixed throughout the whole tissue","In the temporal lobe near the hippocampus, with the peripheral retina given several hundred times more representation than the fovea itself"],"answer":1,"exp":"The primary visual cortex lies in the calcarine fissure of the occipital lobe; the macula (which includes the fovea) has several hundred times more cortical representation than an equivalent area of peripheral retina, reflecting its role in high-acuity central vision. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"How are signals from the two eyes organized within the primary visual cortex, in contrast to the LGN?","choices":["Only one eye's signal ever reaches the visual cortex, since the other eye's fibers terminate entirely within the superior colliculus","Signals from the two eyes are averaged together directly within the LGN itself, arriving at the cortex as a single, already-fused image","Separation of signals from the two eyes is lost — signals from one eye enter every other cortical column, alternating with the other eye, allowing the cortex to compare the two images","They remain completely segregated by cortical column exactly as they already were in the LGN, with no mixing of any kind occurring anywhere within the cortex"],"answer":2,"exp":"Unlike the LGN, where signals from the two eyes remain segregated by layer, this separation is lost in the primary visual cortex — signals from one eye enter every other cortical column, alternating with signals from the other eye, allowing the cortex to determine whether the two eyes' signals match (important for stereopsis). — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What three types of information are thought to be processed by parallel pathways in visual perception?","choices":["Only pain and temperature, unrelated to vision","Motion, depth/form, and color","Only color information, with no separate pathways for motion or form","Taste, smell, and touch"],"answer":1,"exp":"Visual perception is thought to be mediated by three parallel processing pathways handling motion, depth and form, and color — though exactly how the brain integrates these into a unified conscious visual perception is not well understood. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What is the primary visual cortex mainly concerned with analyzing in the visual scene?","choices":["Absolute color values only, with dedicated neurons for each specific wavelength but no attention to contrast borders","Only the overall brightness of the entire visual field, averaged across the retina with no spatial detail preserved","Contrasts in the visual scene, including the sharpness and orientation of contrast borders — with specific neurons stimulated by specific line orientations","Sound localisation cues relayed directly from the inferior colliculus, integrated together with retinal signal timing information"],"answer":2,"exp":"The visual signal reaching the primary visual cortex is concerned mainly with contrasts in the visual scene; sharper contrast produces greater stimulation, and the cortex also detects the direction/orientation of each line and border, with specific neurons responding to specific line orientations. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which subcortical structure receives retinal input responsible for the pupillary light reflex and lens accommodation?","choices":["The pretectal nuclei","The ventral lateral geniculate nucleus","The superior colliculus","The suprachiasmatic nucleus"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pretectal nuclei receive retinal projections responsible for the pupillary light reflex and accommodation of the lens, distinct from the suprachiasmatic nucleus (circadian rhythm control) and superior colliculus (rapid eye movement control). — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which subcortical structure receives retinal input thought to control circadian rhythms?","choices":["The superior colliculus within the midbrain","The primary visual cortex directly","The suprachiasmatic nucleus of the hypothalamus","The pretectal nuclei of the midbrain"],"answer":2,"exp":"The suprachiasmatic nucleus of the hypothalamus receives direct retinal projections thought to be involved in the control of circadian rhythms. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What retinal-projection-related function does the superior colliculus serve?","choices":["Regulation of pupillary constriction alone","Slow production of aqueous humor","Rapid directional movement of both eyes","Long-term control of circadian rhythms"],"answer":2,"exp":"The superior colliculus receives retinal projections and is involved in the rapid directional movement of both eyes, and also contributes to the negative-feedback mechanism that keeps objects of attention fixed on the fovea. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which pair of extraocular muscles is responsible for rotating the eyes, as opposed to moving them side to side or up and down?","choices":["Superior and inferior recti","None of the extraocular muscles rotate the eye","Medial and lateral recti","Superior and inferior obliques"],"answer":3,"exp":"The superior and inferior oblique muscles rotate the eyes, while the medial and lateral recti move the eyes side to side, and the superior and inferior recti move the eyes up and down. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What controls voluntary fixation movements of the eyes, versus the involuntary fixation mechanism?","choices":["Voluntary fixation requires no cortical involvement of any kind whatsoever, arising entirely as a brainstem reflex that is generated by the pretectal nuclei acting directly through the superior colliculus","Both voluntary and involuntary fixation are controlled by the exact same area of premotor cortex, with no separate involvement from the occipital lobe or superior colliculus at all","Involuntary fixation is controlled by the cerebellum exclusively, coordinating eye position purely through direct connections to the vestibular nuclei alone, with no cortical input","Voluntary fixation is controlled by an area in the premotor cortex; involuntary fixation (\"locking on\" to an object) is controlled by secondary visual areas of the occipital cortex, via negative feedback at the level of the superior colliculus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Voluntary fixation movements are controlled by an area in the premotor cortex, while the involuntary fixation mechanism (which \"locks\" the eyes onto an object of attention found via voluntary fixation) is controlled by secondary visual areas of the occipital cortex, using negative feedback at the level of the superior colliculus to keep the object of attention on the fovea. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"From what brainstem nucleus do parasympathetic fibers controlling the ciliary muscle and pupillary sphincter originate?","choices":["The suprachiasmatic nucleus, synapsing directly in the retina","The nucleus tractus solitarius, synapsing in the pterygopalatine ganglion","The Edinger-Westphal nucleus, synapsing in the ciliary ganglion","The superior cervical ganglion, synapsing near the internal carotid artery"],"answer":2,"exp":"Parasympathetic preganglionic fibers controlling eye function arise from the Edinger-Westphal nucleus (associated with CN III, the oculomotor nerve) and synapse with postganglionic fibers in the ciliary ganglion, which then control the ciliary muscle (lens focusing) and the sphincter of the iris (pupil constriction). — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Where do sympathetic preganglionic fibers to the eye originate, and what do the postganglionic fibers innervate?","choices":["They originate in the intermediolateral horn of the first thoracic spinal segment, synapse in the superior cervical ganglion, and innervate the radial fibers of the iris (which open the pupil) plus several extraocular muscles","They originate instead in the Edinger-Westphal nucleus, synapse within the ciliary ganglion, and innervate the circular fibers of the iris along with the ciliary muscle","They originate instead within the pterygopalatine ganglion and innervate only the lacrimal gland itself, with no connection whatsoever to iris tone or to extraocular muscle function at all","Sympathetic fibers have essentially no role in eye innervation at all, since both pupil dilation and constriction are mediated entirely by parasympathetic pathways alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Sympathetic preganglionic fibers to the eye originate in the intermediolateral horn of the first thoracic spinal cord segment and synapse with postganglionic fibers in the superior cervical ganglion; these postganglionic fibers innervate the radial fibers of the iris (opening the pupil, mydriasis) plus several extraocular muscles. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What effect does increased light on the retina have on pupil diameter, and what nerve/nuclei mediate this?","choices":["Light itself has no direct effect on pupil diameter at all, which instead varies only with the degree of lens accommodation used for near objects","Pupillary constriction is mediated entirely by the sympathetic nervous system, acting directly upon the radial fibres of the iris via alpha-adrenergic receptors","Increased light causes pupillary dilation via sympathetic stimulation of the radial iris muscle, triggered through the superior cervical ganglion","Increased light causes pupillary constriction (miosis) via parasympathetic stimulation of the pupillary sphincter, triggered by impulses passing from the optic nerve to the pretectal nuclei"],"answer":3,"exp":"Increased light entering the eye causes pupillary constriction (miosis) via parasympathetic excitation of the pupillary sphincter muscle, triggered by impulses passing from the optic nerve to the pretectal nuclei (the pupillary light reflex) — helping the eyes adapt to rapid changes in light conditions. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"How does chromatic aberration provide a cue for the accommodation control mechanism?","choices":["Red and blue light focus at exactly the same point in perfectly emmetropic eyes, providing no useful cue except in cases of astigmatism","Only infrared light contributes in any meaningful way to accommodation cues, since it consistently focuses furthest in front of the retinal surface","Red light rays focus posteriorly compared to blue light rays; the eye can detect which color is better focused and use this information to help drive the accommodation response","Chromatic aberration has no relationship to accommodation, which is driven solely by convergence signals from the extraocular muscles"],"answer":2,"exp":"Because of chromatic aberration, red light rays focus slightly posterior to blue light rays; the eye can detect which color appears in better focus and relay this information to help drive the accommodation mechanism toward the correct lens adjustment. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is the difference between binocular and stereoscopic vision?","choices":["They are identical terms describing the fusion of retinal images that occurs within the lateral geniculate nucleus","Binocular vision means seeing an object with both eyes simultaneously; stereoscopic vision means seeing objects as three-dimensional","Binocular vision refers to perceiving depth through motion parallax; stereoscopic vision refers to peripheral field width","Neither term relates to eye function; both instead describe auditory localization of sound sources"],"answer":1,"exp":"Binocular vision refers to seeing an object with both eyes simultaneously, while stereoscopic vision refers to perceiving objects as three-dimensional — made possible because slightly different images from each eye are fused in the brain into a single 3-D image. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"How does eye placement differ between predatory animals (e.g., hawks, lions) and animals that are preyed upon (e.g., rabbits), and what functional tradeoff does this represent?","choices":["Both predatory and prey animals have independently evolved identical eye placement and identical visual field width, since binocular overlap is thought to confer no meaningful survival advantage to either group","Eye placement has no functional consequence for either group at all, since visual acuity and color perception matter far more for hunting success and predator detection than field-of-view geometry","Predators have eyes on the sides of the head for a wide field of view useful in tracking multiple prey animals at once; prey species instead have forward-facing eyes that sacrifice peripheral vision for stereoscopic depth judgment","Predators have forward-facing eyes providing good binocular/stereoscopic vision (useful for judging distance to prey); prey animals have eyes on the sides of the head giving a wide field of view (good for detecting movement/threats) but poor stereoscopic vision"],"answer":3,"exp":"Predatory animals (hawks, lions) have forward-facing eyes, giving good binocular and stereoscopic vision useful for judging distance to prey; animals that are preyed upon (rabbits) have eyes on the sides of the head, giving a wide field of view good for detecting movement/threats but providing poor stereoscopic vision. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Approximately what fixed visual angle do human eyes have, and how does close eye spacing contribute to distance judgment?","choices":["104 degrees, with overlapping visual fields from closely spaced eyes allowing accurate judgment of distance","About 200 degrees, though widely spaced eyes give the two visual fields almost no overlap","About 30 degrees, with eyes spaced so closely that the two fields overlap almost completely","The visual angle is fixed near 60 degrees, entirely independent of interpupillary spacing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Humans have a fixed visual angle of about 104 degrees; because the eyes are close together, their visual fields overlap substantially, which allows for accurate judgment of distance via binocular/stereoscopic cues. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"How does eye convergence relate to the accommodation (lens-focusing) mechanism?","choices":["Convergence and accommodation are entirely unrelated processes, controlled by completely separate and non-overlapping cranial nerve pathways","The neural signal for convergence causes a simultaneous signal that weakens the lens, decreasing its overall refractive power","Convergence only occurs several seconds after accommodation is fully complete, with the two processes never overlapping in time","The neural signal for convergence (turning the eyes inward for near objects) causes a simultaneous signal that strengthens the lens (increases accommodation)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The neural signal driving eye convergence (turning both eyes inward to view a near object) also triggers a simultaneous signal that strengthens the lens, coupling convergence and accommodation together for near vision. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Does the degree of lens accommodation stay perfectly fixed once an image is in focus?","choices":["Oscillation only occurs when viewing extremely close objects, at a slow rate of roughly once every ten seconds","Yes — once an image has come into focus the lens shape locks permanently until the convergence angle changes substantially","Accommodation only oscillates in young children, stopping completely by around age 20 as the lens stiffens","No — oscillation of the degree of accommodation occurs continuously (about twice per second), helping the system detect and maintain the best focus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The degree of accommodation oscillates continuously (about twice per second) even when an image appears to be in focus; this oscillation helps the visual system detect whether an image becomes better or worse focused as lens thickness changes, guiding fine-tuning of accommodation. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"How might the fovea's position within a depression of the retina contribute to accommodation control?","choices":["The fovea only provides fine visual acuity information, entirely unrelated to accommodation or lens focus","The fovea sits at the same depth as the surrounding retina, so no depth-based cue can be extracted from its position","It has no relationship to accommodation control, which instead relies solely on chromatic aberration cues","Differences between the foveal image and the image of the surrounding retina may give clues about which direction the lens should adjust"],"answer":3,"exp":"Because the fovea lies in a slight depression of the retina, differences between the image focused on the fovea and the image on the surrounding retina may provide additional clues about which direction (thicker or thinner) the lens should adjust during accommodation. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Approximately how many different functional types of retinal ganglion cells exist, and what kinds of stimulus features can different types respond to?","choices":["Only 2 distinct types exist, one responding exclusively to light onset and the other exclusively to light offset","More than 20 different types, responding to specific directions of motion/orientation, fine detail, increases/decreases in light, or particular colors","Exactly 3 distinct types exist, corresponding directly one-to-one with the 3 subtypes of bipolar cells feeding into them","Ganglion cells are functionally identical with no diversity at all, since every bit of specialisation occurs earlier at the photoreceptor level"],"answer":1,"exp":"There are more than 20 different functional types of retinal ganglion cells, with different types responding to specific directions of motion or orientation, fine detail, increases or decreases in light, or particular colors — encoding many different aspects of the visual scene before signals even leave the eye. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Is the mechanism by which the brain assembles a coherent conscious visual perception from parallel processing pathways fully understood?","choices":["Only motion perception remains unexplained; both the color and depth pathways are now considered completely mapped and understood","Visual perception requires no neural processing beyond the LGN, with the thalamus alone generating full conscious awareness","No — how the brain actually perceives a visual image is not well understood, despite knowing that three parallel pathways process motion, depth/form, and color","Yes — the exact neural mechanism of visual perception was fully mapped out once the three parallel processing pathways had been identified"],"answer":2,"exp":"How the brain actually perceives a coherent visual image is not well understood, even though it is known that visual perception involves three parallel pathways processing motion, depth and form, and color — visual perception is described as a \"creative process\" that goes beyond simple signal relay. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 42"}]};
